---
title: "SSRM Refresh"
enterprise: true
---

This section demonstrates refreshing rows in order to reflect changes at the source while using the Server-Side Row Model (SSRM).

{% note %}
It is advised to use [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) when using Server-Side Refresh. Row IDs allow the grid to retain row state between refreshes, such as row height, expanded state, and cell flashing.
{% /note %}

## Refresh API

The grid API `refreshServerSide(params)` instructs the grid to start reloading all loaded rows for a specified group.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["refreshServerSide"] config={"overrideBottomMargin":"0rem"} /%}

## Simple Example

To ensure your grid reflects the latest data on your server, you can periodically instruct the grid to refresh all of the loaded rows (known as polling) or strategically refresh based on your applications requirements.

The following example provides a simple demonstration of the different behaviours of the refresh API, note the following:

- Using the **Refresh Rows** button, you can request that all the rows are requested from the server again, bringing them up to date with the server version.
- Because [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) have been implemented, the grid is able to detect which rows have been updated, and flash cells when using `enableCellChangeFlash`.
- The `Purge` checkbox enables the purge option in the API call, this causes all rows (and all row state except row selection state) to be reset when the refresh call is made, and replaced with loading rows.
- When a refresh is finished, note the `storeRefreshed` event is fired, and logged in the console. This is not fired when the purge option is enabled as the rows are reset not refreshed.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Simple Example" name="refreshing-the-grid"  exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Refreshing Groups

When using row grouping with refreshing you are required to provide a route parameter specifying the row group to refresh. When a row group is refreshed, only its direct child rows are refreshed. This means that in order to refresh the rows in a particular row group, you need to provide the parent of the rows to be refreshed as the route parameter.

The following example demonstrates how to refresh specified groups on the server, note the following:

- Using the **Refresh Root Level** button, you can force all the rows in the root level group to refresh, this is equivalent to omitting a route parameter from the `refreshServerSide` API call.
- The **Refresh \['Canada'] Group** button only refreshes the direct children of the `Canada` row group.
- The **Refresh \['Canada', '2002'] Group** button only refreshes the direct children of the `2002` row group that belongs to the `Canada` row group.
- Because [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) have been implemented, the grid is able to retain the state for reloaded rows, such as whether a group row was expanded.
- When a refresh is finished, note the `storeRefreshed` event is fired, and logged in the console.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Refreshing Groups" name="refreshing-the-groups"  exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Level Consistency

Each block of a level is loaded by a separate `getRows` request. If the data on the server changes between these requests, for example rows are inserted, deleted or reordered by a sort on a frequently updated column, rows can be duplicated or dropped across a block boundary.

Enable `serverSideCheckLevelConsistency` to have the grid detect this. Note the following:

- Each block request asks for one extra row, i.e. `endRow` is one past the end of the block. The extra row is compared with the first row of the next block and is never displayed. The datasource must therefore return rows up to `endRow`.
- [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) are required, as rows are compared by their ID.
- Each time a level whose blocks disagree finishes loading, the grid fires `serverSideLevelInconsistent` once for that level, even while other levels are still loading. The event contains the `route` of the level and an `inconsistencies` entry for each affected block boundary, with its `type` (`duplicated` or `dropped`), `boundaryIndex` and the `rowIds` involved.
- When a row is returned twice, the grid moves it to its latest position and reloads the block it was previously in, so a `duplicated` boundary may already be resolved by the time the event fires. Dropped rows are not recovered. It is up to the application to decide how to respond.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-options/properties.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["serverSideCheckLevelConsistency"] config={"overrideBottomMargin":"0rem"} /%}

The following snippet reports inconsistencies to the application rather than reloading on every event:

```js
const gridOptions = {
    rowModelType: 'serverSide',
    getRowId: (params) => params.data.id,
    serverSideCheckLevelConsistency: true,
    onServerSideLevelInconsistent: (event) => {
        // e.g. log, record a metric or tell the user the view may be out of date
        console.warn('Level inconsistent', event.route, event.inconsistencies);
    },
};
```

Avoid calling `refreshServerSide` each time the event fires. Reloading is itself a series of block requests, so while the data keeps changing it is likely to be inconsistent again. If a reload is used at all, limit the number of attempts, for example per route. To reduce the chance of inconsistencies:

- Use a larger `cacheBlockSize`, so that a level is read in a single request.
- Avoid server-side sorting on a column that is updated frequently.

The following example demonstrates level consistency checking, note the following:

- The `cacheBlockSize` is `10`, and the server alternately deletes and inserts a row at the top of the data before each block after the first is requested, so each newly loaded block is inconsistent with the block above it.
- Each `serverSideLevelInconsistent` event is logged to the console, and summarised above the grid. Deleted rows are reported as `dropped`, inserted rows as `duplicated`.
- Scroll down to load further blocks and report further inconsistencies.
- Untick **Change data between block requests** and click **Reload** to load the level from unchanging data. No inconsistencies are reported.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Level Consistency" name="level-consistency" exampleHeight=615 /%}
